let the URL tools do the untangling:

```javascript
function getQuery(url) {
  return new URL(url).searchParams.get('q') ?? '';
}
```

`getQuery('https://example.com/?q=red+door')` returns `'red door'`. A missing `q` returns `''`. If `q` occurs more than once, this returns its first value. An invalid URL throws, so this version assumes the input is a valid absolute URL.

we can give each run its own little parcel:

```python
def group_runs(values):
    runs = []
    for value in values:
        if runs and runs[-1][0] == value:
            previous, count = runs[-1]
            runs[-1] = (previous, count + 1)
        else:
            runs.append((value, 1))
    return runs

assert group_runs(iter(['a', 'a', 'b', 'a'])) == [
    ('a', 2), ('b', 1), ('a', 1)
]
```

Only the most recent run is compared, so the final `'a'` starts a new pair. This consumes the iterable once and returns a list. Values need equality comparison, not hashing. Time is linear in the number of items, assuming equality checks take constant time.

a `Map` is a tidy fit: updating an existing key changes its value without moving that key's place.

```javascript
function latestById(records) {
  const byId = new Map();
  for (const record of records) {
    byId.set(record.id, record);
  }
  return [...byId.values()];
}

const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
const records = [
  {id: 'b', title: 'First'},
  {id: 'a', title: 'Second'},
  {id: 'b', title: 'Revised'}
];
assert.deepEqual(latestById(records), [
  {id: 'b', title: 'Revised'},
  {id: 'a', title: 'Second'}
]);
assert.deepEqual(latestById([]), []);
assert.equal(records[0].title, 'First');
```

The test runs in Node.js. The function doesn't change the input array or its records. It returns a new array containing the original record objects, not deep copies. Assume every record has a valid `id`; numeric `1` and string `'1'` are different keys. Last record, first place. a small orderly compromise.
